{
	"nodes": [
		{
			"id": "header",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## Computational Neuroscience and Brain Theories\n\nDomain hub: formal models of neural computation - coding, dynamics, learning, inference - and the theories of brain function built on them.\n\n[[Computational Neuroscience and Brain Theories Map|Open the domain map]] - snapshot of 2026-09-25; the map's *In this folder* section is the live view.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 0,
			"width": 1680,
			"height": 170,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "g0",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Foundations",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 1242
		},
		{
			"id": "s0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Computational Neuroscience and Brain Theories - Foundations|Foundations index]]**",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 88,
			"color": "#B8A4FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Dynamical systems models]]**\n*theory* - 4 sources\n\nDescribing neural activity as trajectories through a state space, and what such models do and do not explain.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 508,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 194,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Levels of analysis]]**\n*framework* - 7 sources\n\nWhy the same system is described at molecular, cellular, circuit, cognitive and behavioural levels, and what each level can and cannot explain.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 762,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Model comparison and identifiability]]**\n*method* - 4 sources\n\nHow competing models are compared, why a good fit is weak evidence, and what parameter recovery adds.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 1040,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 194,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Neural coding and population codes]]**\n*theory* - 5 sources\n\nHow information is represented in firing patterns: rate, timing and population-level descriptions, and the evidence that distinguishes them.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 1294,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "g1",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Learning and inference",
			"x": 420,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 1544
		},
		{
			"id": "s1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Computational Neuroscience and Brain Theories - Learning and inference|Learning and inference index]]**",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 88,
			"color": "#B8A4FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Active inference and free energy]]**\n*theory* - 5 sources\n\nA framework that treats action, perception and learning as minimising a single quantity, and the criticisms that scope raises.",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 508,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Bayesian inference and predictive processing]]**\n*theory* - 3 sources\n\nPerception as probabilistic inference, the predictive-coding family of algorithms, and the differences between them.",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 786,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Model-based and model-free control]]**\n*theory* - 4 sources\n\nTwo ways of choosing actions - cached values versus an internal model of the world - and how their signatures are measured.",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 1064,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 194,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Reinforcement learning]]**\n*theory* - 4 sources\n\nLearning from reward: value functions, policies, the exploration-exploitation trade-off, and why the framework fits neural data.",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 1318,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_4",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Reward prediction error]]**\n*theory* - 4 sources\n\nThe signal proposed to drive learning from reward, its neural correlates, and the conditions under which the mapping is disputed.",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 1596,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "g2",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Networks and theories",
			"x": 840,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 988
		},
		{
			"id": "s2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Computational Neuroscience and Brain Theories - Networks and theories|Networks and theories index]]**",
			"x": 870,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 88,
			"color": "#B8A4FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Global workspace and integrated information]]**\n*theory* - 5 sources\n\nTwo influential theories of consciousness, their core commitments and their different predictions.",
			"x": 870,
			"y": 508,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Network and connectome models]]**\n*theory* - 7 sources\n\nDescribing the brain as a network: nodes, edges, topology and dynamics, and the assumptions these descriptions carry.",
			"x": 870,
			"y": 786,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 194,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Theories of consciousness compared]]**\n*framework* - 6 sources\n\nHow to compare theories of consciousness: shared commitments, differing predictions, and the problem of testing them against each other.",
			"x": 870,
			"y": 1040,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#7C7CF5"
		},
		{
			"id": "g3",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Most-cited evidence",
